1
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:06,310
Ed: Did you make notes while you were watching the performances of Romeo and Juliet, Gemma?

2
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:09,820
Gemma: Yes, I did. I found it quite hard though

3
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:12,240
I kept getting too involved in the play.

4
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:15,002
Ed: Me too. I ended up not taking notes.

5
00:00:15,002 --> 00:00:17,880
I wrote down my impressions when I got home

6
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:21,140
Do you mind if I check a few things with you?

7
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:26,050
In case I've missed anything. And I've also got some questions about our assignment.

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,950
Gemma: No, it's good to talk things through. I may have missed things too.

9
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:33,840
Ed: OK great. So first of all

10
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:37,990
I'm not sure how much information we should include in our reviews.

11
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:42,830
Gemma: Right. Well, I don't think we need to describe what happens.

12
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:47,670
Especially as Romeo and Juliet is one of Shakespeare's most well-known plays.

13
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:49,797
Ed: Yeah, everyone knows the story.

14
00:00:49,797 --> 00:00:55,467
In an essay we'd focus on the poetry and Shakespeare's use of imagery etc.,

15
00:00:55,467 --> 00:00:58,657
but that isn't really relevant in a review.

16
00:00:58,657 --> 00:01:02,910
We're supposed to focus on how effective this particular production is.

17
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:07,740
Gemma: Mmm. We should say what made it a success or a failure.

18
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:12,433
Ed: And part of that means talking about the emotional impact the performance had on us.

19
00:01:12,433 --> 00:01:13,900
I think that's important.

20
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:20,952
Gemma: Yes. And we should definitely mention how well the director handled important bits of the play -

21
00:01:20,952 --> 00:01:24,270
like when Romeo climbs onto Juliet's balcony.

22
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:26,670
Ed: And the fight between Mercutio and Tybalt.

23
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:30,646
Gemma: Yes. It would also be interesting to mention the theatre space

24
00:01:30,646 --> 00:01:36,610
and how the director used it but I don't think we'll have space in 800 words.

25
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:43,900
Ed: No. OK. That all sounds quite straightforward.

26
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,870
Ed: So what about The Emporium Theatre's production of the play?

27
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:52,730
Gemma: I thought some things worked really well but there were some problems too.

28
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:56,010
Ed: Yeah. What about the set, for example?

29
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:58,833
Gemma: I think it was visually really stunning.

30
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:03,420
I'd say that was probably the most memorable thing about this production.

31
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:07,053
Ed: You're right. The set design was really amazing,

32
00:02:07,053 --> 00:02:11,050
but actually I have seen similar ideas used in other productions.

33
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:15,240
Gemma: What about the lighting? Some of the scenes were

34
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,960
so dimly lit it was quite hard to see.

35
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:23,240
Ed: I didn't dislike it. It helped to change the mood of the quieter scenes.

36
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:24,810
Gemma: That's a good point.

37
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:26,890
Ed: What did you think of the costumes?

38
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:30,710
Gemma: I was a bit surprised by the contemporary dress, I must say.

39
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:35,200
Ed: Yeah - I think it worked well, but I had assumed it would be more conventional.

40
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:38,440
Gemma: Me too. I liked the music at the beginning

41
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,881
and I thought the musicians were brilliant,

42
00:02:40,881 --> 00:02:43,300
but I thought they were wasted

43
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:47,230
because the music didn't have much impact in Acts 2 and 3.

44
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:49,090
Ed: Yes - that was a shame.

45
00:02:49,090 --> 00:02:54,298
Gemma: One problem with this production was that the actors didn't deliver the lines that well.

46
00:02:54,298 --> 00:02:55,830
They were speaking too fast.

47
00:02:55,830 --> 00:02:57,801
Ed: It was a problem I agree,

48
00:02:57,801 --> 00:03:00,720
but I thought it was because they weren't speaking loudly enough

49
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:03,150
especially at key points in the play.

50
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:06,130
Gemma: I actually didn't have a problem with that.

51
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:11,280
Ed: It's been an interesting experience watching different versions of Romeo and Juliet, hasn't it?

52
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:15,720
Gemma: Definitely. It's made me realise how relevant the play still is.

53
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:18,840
Ed: Right. I mean a lot's changed since Shakespeare's time

54
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:22,041
but in many ways nothing's changed.

55
00:03:22,041 --> 00:03:25,700
There are always disagreements and tension between teenagers and their parents.

56
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:29,580
Gemma: Yes, that's something all young people can relate to -

57
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:33,460
more than the violence and the extreme emotions in the play.

58
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:36,760
Ed: How did you find watching it in translation?

59
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:40,720
Gemma: Really interesting. I expected to find it more challenging

60
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:43,230
but I could follow the story pretty well.

61
00:03:43,230 --> 00:03:47,612
Ed: I stopped worrying about not being able to understand all the words

62
00:03:47,612 --> 00:03:51,320
and focused on the actors' expressions. The ending was pretty powerful.

63
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:55,700
Gemma: Yes. That somehow intensified the emotion for me.

64
00:03:55,700 --> 00:04:00,880
Ed: Did you know Shakespeare's been translated into more languages than any other writer?

65
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:05,440
Gemma: What's the reason for his international appeal, do you think?

66
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,280
Ed: I was reading that it's

67
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:11,940
because his plays are about basic themes that people everywhere are familiar with.

68
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:16,040
Gemma: Yeah, and they can also be understood on different levels

69
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,110
The characters have such depth.

70
00:04:18,110 --> 00:04:22,600
Ed: Right - which allows directors to experiment and find new angles.

71
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,600
Gemma: That's really important because...
